<%# locals: (statement:, period:, as_of:, performance:, returns:) %>

<%# What the portfolio RETURNED over the period, as rates rather than amounts.
    The KPI row above answers "how much"; this answers "how well", which is the
    figure a reader compares against a broker statement or another tracker.

    `returns` is the figure set the registry read off Portfolio::Performance for
    this one period; this partial only formats. Every figure may legitimately be
    nil -- see the disclosure note below -- so each card is allowed to say so
    without the section disappearing. %>
<div id="portfolio-performance" class="space-y-4">
  <%# A figure withheld is not the same as a figure of zero, and the reader
      cannot tell them apart from the cards alone. R13 suppresses a figure whose
      exchange rate is missing; R6 suppresses a day whose denominator is not
      positive. Both are surfaced rather than hidden, which is what the contract
      means by "suppressed days are surfaced". %>
  <% if performance.rate_missing? || performance.suppressed_dates.any? %>
    <div class="px-4">
      <%= render DS::Alert.new(title: t("portfolios.performance.incomplete_title"), variant: :warning) do %>
        <ul class="text-xs text-secondary space-y-1">
          <% if performance.rate_missing? %>
            <li><%= t("portfolios.performance.rate_missing") %></li>
          <% end %>
          <% if performance.suppressed_dates.any? %>
            <li><%= t("portfolios.performance.suppressed_days", count: performance.suppressed_dates.count) %></li>
          <% end %>
        </ul>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
    <%# The method matters as much as the number. Users compare these against a
        broker, and brokers do not agree on a convention -- this engine uses a
        start-of-day flow convention, so a tool defaulting to another method
        gives a different figure for the same account. The hint says which. %>
    <%= render "portfolios/kpi_card",
          icon: "trending-up",
          label: t("portfolios.performance.twr"),
          money: nil,
          rate: returns[:twr],
          hint: t("portfolios.performance.twr_hint") %>

    <%= render "portfolios/kpi_card",
          icon: "calendar",
          label: t("portfolios.performance.annualized_twr"),
          money: nil,
          rate: returns[:annualized_twr],
          hint: t("portfolios.performance.annualized_hint") %>

    <%# Withheld entirely for a valuation-only scope (R16): without trade or
        transfer records the external flows are unknown, and an XIRR over them
        would be a fabrication. Nil arrives here and reads as no data. %>
    <%= render "portfolios/kpi_card",
          icon: "user",
          label: t("portfolios.performance.mwr"),
          money: nil,
          rate: returns[:mwr],
          hint: t("portfolios.performance.mwr_hint") %>

    <%= render "portfolios/kpi_card",
          icon: "calendar-clock",
          label: t("portfolios.performance.annualized_mwr"),
          money: nil,
          rate: returns[:annualized_mwr],
          hint: t("portfolios.performance.annualized_hint") %>

    <%= render "portfolios/kpi_card",
          icon: "activity",
          label: t("portfolios.performance.volatility"),
          money: nil,
          rate: returns[:volatility],
          hint: t("portfolios.performance.volatility_hint") %>

    <%= render "portfolios/kpi_card",
          icon: "trending-down",
          label: t("portfolios.performance.max_drawdown"),
          money: nil,
          rate: returns[:max_drawdown],
          hint: t("portfolios.performance.max_drawdown_hint") %>
  </div>
</div>
